e margin could narrow considerably.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Dan McKee (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island's governorship is open this year after Dan McKee lost the Democratic primary; Helena Foulkes now carries the party's banner against Republican Aaron Guckian and independents including Ken Block. The state leans Democratic by about eight points, and all seven major race ratings place it in the Solid or Safe Democratic column.\n\nPolls since the primary show Foulkes ahead of Guckian by roughly 22 to 27 points, and prediction markets put Democratic chances near 97 percent. Guckian has struggled for visibility, skipping an October debate while courting national Republican support in Florida. Block is campaigning as a change agent and urging a two-way race, splitting opposition votes rather than consolidating them. Unless he can turn the contest into a genuine head-to-head, Foulkes remains a heavy favorite.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 18.1}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.997, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9964, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9941, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9953, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9924, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9959, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9963, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.995, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.995, "margin": 20.0}]}